## 解答

**S1 — Recall the $myb72^{-/-}$ baseline (sub_prev).**

From the prior step, in the $myb72^{-/-}$ single mutant under phosphate deficiency:
- Severed arrow: $MYB72 \xrightarrow{\text{enzymatic\_induction}} F6'H1$
- Unreachable nodes: $\{F6'H1, Coumarins, \Delta Microbiota\}$
- Preserved upstream activity: $PHR1 \xrightarrow{\text{transcriptional\_activation}} MYB72^{\varnothing}$ (PHR1 is active but signal cannot propagate past $MYB72^{\varnothing}$)
- $\Delta Microbiota$ is absent; MYB72 is necessary for the downstream sub-chain.

**S2 — Apply the $phr1$ lesion on top of the $myb72$ lesion (double mutant).**

In the $myb72^{-/-}$ single mutant, the arrow $PHR1 \xrightarrow{\text{transcriptional\_activation}} MYB72$ was structurally intact (PHR1 was active and did signal toward $MYB72$), even though $MYB72$ was non-functional. In the double mutant $phr1^{-/-}\,myb72^{-/-}$, PHR1 itself is completely non-functional, severing that first arrow at its source.

**(i) ADDITIONAL_SEVERED_ARROW:**
The $phr1$ lesion severs $PHR1 \xrightarrow{\text{transcriptional\_activation}} MYB72$. This arrow was present (though producing only $MYB72^{\varnothing}$) in the $myb72^{-/-}$ single-mutant baseline; it is now severed at the source in the double mutant. This is the one additional severed arrow relative to the $myb72^{-/-}$ baseline.

**(ii) UNREACHABLE_NODES:**
With PHR1 non-functional, no signal enters the chain at any node. Every node downstream of PHR1 — i.e., $\{MYB72, F6'H1, Coumarins, \Delta Microbiota\}$ — is unreachable. (In the $myb72^{-/-}$ single mutant, MYB72 was nominally reached as a target but produced no output; in the double mutant, MYB72 is also unreachable as a target of PHR1.)

**(iii) PRESERVED_UPSTREAM_ACTIVITY:**
None. PHR1 itself is non-functional. There is no active node in the chain; no upstream signaling activity is preserved.

**(iv) EPISTASIS_CLASSIFICATION:**
Compare the double mutant to each single mutant with respect to $\Delta Microbiota$:
- **vs. $myb72^{-/-}$:** $\Delta Microbiota$ is absent in both. Adding the $phr1$ lesion to $myb72^{-/-}$ produces no additional change in $\Delta Microbiota$. The $myb72$ lesion already fully occludes the downstream phenotype; the $phr1$ lesion is redundant with respect to $\Delta Microbiota$. This is linear occlusion epistasis.
- **vs. $phr1^{-/-}$:** $\Delta Microbiota$ is absent in both (the $phr1^{-/-}$ single mutant cannot activate MYB72, so the chain is already broken at the first arrow). Adding the $myb72$ lesion to $phr1^{-/-}$ likewise produces no additional change in $\Delta Microbiota$.
- **Epistasis direction:** PHR1 is upstream of MYB72 in the strictly linear chain. The $phr1$ lesion is epistatically upstream; the $myb72$ lesion is epistatically downstream. Because both single mutants already abolish $\Delta Microbiota$, the double mutant is phenotypically equivalent to either single mutant — a classic linear-pathway occlusion epistasis pattern.

**(v) LOGICAL_CONCLUSION:**
$\text{NECESSARY}(PHR1)$ for transcriptional activation of MYB72; $\text{NECESSARY}(MYB72)$ for enzymatic induction of F6'H1 and the downstream sub-chain $\{F6'H1 \xrightarrow{\text{biosynthesis}} Coumarins \xrightarrow{\text{rhizosphere\_secretion}} \Delta Microbiota\}$. In $phr1^{-/-}\,myb72^{-/-}$, $\Delta Microbiota \equiv$ absent, indistinguishable from either single mutant; no new pathway is unmasked; strict linear epistasis is confirmed with PHR1 upstream of MYB72, and the double-mutant result is fully consistent with the fixed strictly linear topology.

